"A star does not give off just one color. It gives off a whole rainbow of light mixed together, plus light our eyes cannot see. A hotter star gives off more blue light, and a cooler star gives off more red. The Sun's light is strongest in the green part of the rainbow, but it mixes in so much of every other color that the Sun is really white. It only looks yellow or orange when it is low in the sky, because the air scatters away some of its blue light.": "11f8218e9ab2",
"About a hundred years ago, astronomers began sorting stars by their light. At Harvard, Annie Jump Cannon sorted hundreds of thousands of stars into classes named O, B, A, F, G, K, and M, from hottest to coolest. Then Ejnar Hertzsprung and Henry Norris Russell compared each star's color with how bright it really is. Most stars fell along one band, now called the main sequence. A few were giants, far brighter than their color suggests because they are enormous, and a few were tiny, faint white dwarfs.": "340316dcef44",
"After the rain, a rainbow appeared over the mountains.": "ddf4276bc6c0",
"Chinese astronomers paid attention to star colors, too. More than 2,000 years ago, the historian Sima Qian used stars as color samples: white like Sirius, red like Antares, and yellow like Betelgeuse. Yellow? Today Betelgeuse is clearly orange-red. In 2022, a team of astronomers suggested that the ancient records are right and that Betelgeuse has slowly changed color as it aged into a red supergiant. Antares, the red heart of the Scorpion, was known in China as the Great Fire.": "3a365fbc3069",
